+\begin{lemma}
+\label{lemma-reduction-morphism-properties}
+\begin{reference}
+\cite[Proposition 5.1.6]{EGA1}
+\end{reference}
+Let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of schemes and let
+$f_{red} : X_{red} \to Y_{red}$ be the morphism of
+Schemes, Lemma \ref{schemes-lemma-reduction-functorial}.
+Then
+\begin{enumerate}
+\item $f$ is surjective if and only if $f_{red}$ is surjective,
+\item $f$ is universally injective if and only if $f_{red}$ is universally
+injective, and
+\item if $f$ is an immersion, a closed immersion, or an open immersion, then
+the same is true of $f_{red}$.
+\end{enumerate}
+\end{lemma}
+
+\begin{proof}
+The reductions have the same underlying spaces as the original schemes, and
+the vertical maps in the defining square induce isomorphisms on residue
+fields. The first assertion follows immediately. The second follows from
+Lemma \ref{lemma-universally-injective}, which characterizes universal
+injectivity by the map on points and the residue field extensions.
+
+Suppose that $f$ is a closed immersion. Reduction does not change the
+underlying topological map. The induced map on stalks for $f_{red}$ is
+obtained from the surjective stalk map for $f$ by dividing source and target
+by their nilradicals, and hence is surjective. The map on structure sheaves
+is therefore surjective by Sheaves, Lemma
+\ref{sheaves-lemma-characterize-epi-mono}. Thus $f_{red}$ is a closed
+immersion by Schemes, Lemma
+\ref{schemes-lemma-characterize-closed-immersions}. If $f$ is an open
+immersion, then its stalk maps are isomorphisms and its image is open; both
+properties remain true after reduction, so $f_{red}$ is an open immersion.
+Finally, factor an immersion as a closed immersion followed by an open
+immersion. Functoriality of reduction and Schemes, Lemma
+\ref{schemes-lemma-composition-immersion} prove the general immersion case.
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{lemma}
+\label{lemma-reductions-fibre-product}
+\begin{reference}
+\cite[Proposition 5.1.7]{EGA1}
+\end{reference}
+Let $X \to S$ and $Y \to S$ be morphisms of schemes. There is a canonical
+isomorphism
+$$
+X_{red} \times_{S_{red}} Y_{red}
+\longrightarrow
+X_{red} \times_S Y_{red}.
+$$
+Via this isomorphism, the canonical morphism
+$$
+X_{red} \times_{S_{red}} Y_{red} \longrightarrow X \times_S Y
+$$
+is a closed immersion and a universal homeomorphism.
+\end{lemma}
+
+\begin{proof}
+The maps from $X_{red}$ and $Y_{red}$ to $S$ factor through $S_{red}$ by
+Schemes, Lemma \ref{schemes-lemma-reduction-functorial}. Since
+$S_{red} \to S$ is a monomorphism, the two fibre products in the statement
+have the same universal property. This gives the canonical isomorphism.
+
+After replacing the source by $X_{red} \times_S Y_{red}$, the morphism to
+$X \times_S Y$ factors as
+$$
+X_{red} \times_S Y_{red} \longrightarrow
+X \times_S Y_{red} \longrightarrow X \times_S Y.
+$$
+Both arrows are base changes of reduction morphisms, and hence are closed
+immersions by Schemes, Lemma
+\ref{schemes-lemma-fibre-product-immersion}. Their composition is a closed
+immersion by Schemes, Lemma \ref{schemes-lemma-composition-immersion}. Both
+arrows are universal homeomorphisms by Lemmas
+\ref{lemma-reduction-universal-homeomorphism} and
+\ref{lemma-base-change-universal-homeomorphism}; their composition is a
+universal homeomorphism by
+Lemma \ref{lemma-composition-universal-homeomorphism}.
+\end{proof}
